/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Landing gear is classified first by whether it moves.
   - Shock absorbers are classed by what stores the landing energy: a rigid gear leaves it to the tyres; light aircraft use a spring-steel leg or rubber bungee cords; almost every larger aircraft uses the oleo-pneumatic strut.
   - F is the upward force from the strut in newtons (N), p is the nitrogen pressure in pascals (Pa), and A is the piston area in square metres (m²).
   - Tyres are the only contact with the runway.
   - A hydraulic actuator pushes the gear down or pulls it up; on some aircraft an electric motor drives the same linkage.
   - Sequence keeps the aircraft safe.
   - Gear position indication and the logic that sequences gear with flaps are covered in landing gear and flap control.
   - A brake is an energy converter.
   - E is kinetic energy in joules (J), m is aircraft mass in kilograms (kg), and v is landing speed in metres per second (m/s).
   - On light aircraft each brake pedal works a master cylinder, as in a car.
*/
