/* book exam traps: Swapping the oleo roles: nitrogen gas is the spring, oil is the damper, not the other way round. | Forgetting that brake energy rises with the square of speed: a 10 % faster landing gives 1.1² = 1.21, about 21 % more heat. | Confusing uplock with downlock. The downlock holds the gear rigidly in the down position so the leg cannot fold at touchdown. | Thinking antiskid increases braking pressure. It releases pressure to stop a wheel skidding. | Using the tyre or strut gauge pressure as the force. Force is pressure times piston area, F = pA, and the gas pressure rises as the strut closes, so the static value is only the starting point. */
