/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A starter is a motor that turns the HP compressor through the accessory gearbox.
   - An air turbine starter (ATS) is a small turbine driven by regulated bleed air.
   - An electric starter is a DC series motor, chosen because it gives its highest torque at zero speed.
   - The electrical start circuit keeps a light start switch away from the starter’s current, which can reach several hundred amperes.
   - An ignition exciter takes 28 V DC or 115 V AC and charges a storage capacitor to a few kilovolts.
   - E is the stored energy in joules (J), C is the capacitance in farads (F) and V is the charging voltage in volts (V).
   - Spark energies run from a few joules to about 20 J.
   - In an automatic start the FADEC runs the sequence.
   - Interlocks stop unsafe starts: the start valve will not open with low bleed pressure, fuel is inhibited below a minimum N2, and the igniters are on before fuel arrives, so fuel lights as soon as it enters.
*/
