/* book Worked example: Q: During an auto-start N2 rises at a steady 1.6 % per second while only the starter turns the engine. After light-off, starter and combustion together raise N2 at 2.4 % per second. Ignition comes on at 16 % N2, fuel at 22 % N2 (take light-off as immediate) and the starter cuts out at 50 % N2. Find the time of each event from the start of cranking.
   1. Ignition: t = 16 ÷ 1.6 = 10.0 s.
   2. Fuel and light-off: t = 22 ÷ 1.6 = 13.75 s.
   3. From 22 % to 50 % at 2.4 % per second takes (50 − 22) ÷ 2.4 = 11.67 s.
   4. Cut-out: t = 13.75 + 11.67 = 25.42 s.
   5. Check the order: 10.0 s < 13.75 s < 25.4 s, so the igniters are already sparking when fuel arrives.
   Answer: Ignition at 10.0 s, fuel and light-off at 13.75 s, starter cut-out at 25.4 s after cranking began. */
